# core v3.7 — NEGATIVE. An attachment with no digest. Violation constraint 2 of 3.
#
# The path resolves and the algorithm is named, so this node looks like it
# works, and that is exactly why the constraint is a Violation rather than a
# warning. Without a digest nothing distinguishes the right bytes from any other
# bytes at that path: a re-import that wrote a different document to the same
# filename is undetectable, and the content addressing the whole arrangement
# rests on has quietly become an ordinary file reference.
#
# Note that the path here still ENDS in a digest-shaped segment, because the
# convention is `attachments/{algorithm}/{digest}`. That is not a substitute for
# the property. A path is a string a producer chose; cascade:contentHash is the
# assertion a consumer checks the bytes against, and a shape that read the
# digest out of the filename would be trusting the producer to keep name and
# content in agreement, which is the thing this design refuses to do.
#
# Rejected by cascade:AttachmentShape / cascade:contentHash / sh:minCount 1.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .

<urn:uuid:a77a0307-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a3> a cascade:Attachment ;
    cascade:attachmentPath
        "attachments/sha-256/8dd3c6b5f593b25cb9dc0094d67323d16c3bbc9584eda019726a38dd2cc7a471" ;
    cascade:hashAlgorithm "sha-256" ;
    cascade:attachmentMediaType "application/pdf" ;
    cascade:attachmentTitle "Comprehensive Metabolic Panel — signed report" .
